    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A post office operated in Alder from 1902 until 1975, marking the town’s active years.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alder%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The original town of Alder sat where Alder Lake now lies, and the site was flooded after the town moved out.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ghosttownsofwashington.com/post/old-alder-pierce-county-wa"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "When Alder Lake’s water level drops, the foundations and old roadways of the vanished town become visible.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ghosttownsofwashington.com/post/old-alder-pierce-county-wa"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Construction of the Nisqually Dam began in 1942, and the final concrete was poured in September 1944, completing the project during World War II.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ghosttownsofwashington.com/post/old-alder-pierce-county-wa"
    },
    {
